  "slug": "conformation-asparagine-2e2a",
  "summary": "Recent docking, constant-pH MD, and QM/MM studies map how conformational and protonation states switch enzyme and motor function.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that function depends on the distribution of conformational and protonation states rather than a single static structure. In xanthan glycosyltransferase GumK, AI-based docking with GNINA and AlphaFold3/Boltz cofolding ensembles links conformation to donor selectivity, though this is docking-based rather than direct transition mapping. Constant-pH MD and FEP/pKa calculations on C. jejuni MotAB show asymmetric D22 hydration and protonation switching of dual aspartates driving flagellar rotation. For SARS-CoV-2 nsp16, MD and AI/ML suggest nsp10 activates the enzyme via a hydrophobic latch, while monomeric nsp16 closes its SAM pocket. QM/MM metadynamics and umbrella sampling map the PL42 lyase beta-elimination free-energy surface, implicating His85. What is new is explicit treatment of protonation, hydration, and conformational gating as state-switching elements.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-19"
}
